## 3. Wiki Articles (`wiki/`)

These formats are **machine-parsed by the orchestrators** — `/compile` scans `sources:` to detect unprocessed raw files, `/audit` follows `Wiki Source` paths, `/flight-plan` reads Layer 1 dashboards. Precision is load-bearing: do not improvise field names or drop frontmatter keys.

Every article uses this template:

```markdown
---
title: Human-readable title
domain: <one of the declared wiki_domains>
scope: domain | build | mixed
last_updated: 2026-05-15
sources:
  - raw/2026-05-10-<source>-<description>.md
  - raw/2026-05-12-<source>-<description>.md
cross_links:
  - wiki/<domain>/<other-article>.md
confidence: high | medium | low
---

# Title

Body content. Plain markdown. No tool-specific syntax in the body — keep it portable.

## Sections as needed

Use headers for structure. Keep articles focused on one topic.

## Open Questions

Bullet list of unresolved items. These feed /audit.

## Corrections

Any personnel corrections to compiled content (see § 13 Conflict Resolution).
```

### Naming

- Lowercase, hyphen-separated: `api-endpoints.md`, `customer-flow.md`.
- One article per topic. If an article exceeds ~300 lines, split it.
- Connection articles (cross-domain topics) live at `wiki/connections/TOPIC-A--TOPIC-B.md` (double-hyphen separator) if a `connections` domain is declared in `wiki_domains`.

### Scope Tags

Scope determines which orchestrators care about this article.

| Scope | Meaning | Example |
|-------|---------|---------|
| `domain` | Operational knowledge about how the project's domain works | Vendor relationships, customer patterns, regulatory context |
| `build` | Technical knowledge about systems being built | API endpoints, architecture decisions, system internals |
| `mixed` | Both operational and technical | A system whose operational use is inseparable from its build |

Defaults by subdirectory are declared per-domain in `project.yaml.wiki_domains` (each entry has a `default_scope`). Override the default when the article's actual content doesn't match. The default is a starting point, not a rule.

### Confidence Levels

| Level | Meaning |
|-------|---------|
| `high` | Multiple corroborating sources, or confirmed by direct observation |
| `medium` | Single source or reasonable inference from multiple sources |
| `low` | Educated guess, extrapolation, or single unverified source |

