## 6. Compile Rules (`/compile`)

`/compile` turns raw intake into wiki knowledge. Full workflow lives in `.claude/skills/compile/SKILL.md`; this section documents the routing framework.

### Three-tier routing

**Tier 1 — Explicit `domain:` field (hard directive):** If the raw file's frontmatter contains a `domain:` field, `/compile` writes the resulting wiki article directly into `wiki/<domain>/` with no tag-based inference. The value must be one of the declared `wiki_domains`. An unknown value creates a REVIEW.md entry of type `compile` and the file is skipped.

**Tier 2 — Tag-based inference (fallback):** If the raw file has no `domain:` field, `/compile` routes by tags. Domains classify articles; they do not carry their own tag-set — `project.yaml.wiki_domains` has no `tags` field (see `project.yaml.schema.json`). Tags live in frontmatter: the raw file's own `tags:` field (§ 2 — source tags) and, for engine-managed views, the derivation block's `entities` key (§ 17.1 — entity tags); both are governed by `deploy/entities.yaml` for routing (§ 17.2–17.3), not by a per-domain tag-set the operator declares. One raw file may feed multiple articles across subdirectories when its tags span multiple domains. Tags are the primary signal; content is the tiebreaker.

**Tier 3 — Explicit cross-reference sections (mandatory cascade):** After Tier 1/2 routing, `/compile` reads the raw file body for sections titled "Cross-references to update in next /compile", "Corrections to Existing Wiki", or similar. These sections name specific wiki articles and specific changes. Every listed target is a **mandatory routing destination** — `/compile` must update that article with the specified content, not just the articles found by Tier 1/2 routing.

### Directory Preservation Rule

**No orchestrator may delete, rename, or silently drop a wiki subdirectory without explicit operator authorization.** Adding a new domain never requires removing an existing one. Dropping a domain from the schema is functionally equivalent to deleting it because future compiles will no longer route to it. Authorization is an explicit operator instruction, e.g., "delete wiki/foo". Absence of an instruction is not authorization.

