## 16. Corpus Observation (Execution-Plane Ground Truth)

**Applies only when a corpus binding is declared in `project.yaml`** — either the legacy singular `corpus_source` (not `none`) or the `corpus_sources` list (v3.0.18, backlog v3.0-88). Most projects have no separate execution corpus; for them this section is inert and `/compile` + `/flight-plan` skip their corpus steps. Enable it when this project's *execution plane* — the code being built — lives in one or more **sibling repositories** the wiki should track.

The wiki is the **control plane**; the execution plane is the code. The two repos do **not** auto-sync. Shipped state (what is actually deployed) is **observed from the corpus, never transcribed into `raw/`.**

**Why this is a separate channel from `raw/`:** `raw/` records facts whose only home is a human's head — decisions, rationale, corrections, recon. Shipped state is *not* such a fact; it already exists authoritatively in the corpus (`git log` + the deployed change manifest). Hand-copying it into `raw/` is what drifts. A `raw/` file that merely re-states "X shipped" is the anti-pattern this section prevents.

### Declared ground-truth source(s)

Configured in `project.yaml` (read at runtime by the orchestrators; not init-substituted). Two forms, mutually exclusive:

**Singular (legacy, still valid — one execution repo):**
- `corpus_source` — the `org/repo` identifier of the execution corpus, or `none`
- `corpus_config.clone_path` — local clone used for read-only observation
- `corpus_config.branch` — the deployed branch
- `corpus_config.probe` — default `git log`/HEAD since the last-reconciled SHA; a project may add an enrichment probe (e.g. a build/change manifest).

**List (v3.0.18, backlog v3.0-88 — one entry per execution repo):**
- `corpus_sources[]` — each entry carries `id` (stable short identifier; defaults to the repo name segment of `source`), `source` (`org/repo`), `clone_path`, `branch`, and optional `probe`. Declaring `corpus_sources` alongside a non-`none` `corpus_source` is a **config error** — consumers must refuse it visibly, not pick one.

Consumers build one **effective corpus list** from whichever form is declared (the singular form is a one-entry list whose `id` is the repo name) and then treat every corpus identically and independently. The **last-reconciled SHA lives per corpus** in `wiki/HEALTH.md` § Corpus Observation — one row per corpus, keyed by `id` (written by `/compile`, read by `/flight-plan`). The abstraction is "ground-truth source + probe"; a production-telemetry source would be a future instance of the same shape.

### The loop (sense → reconcile → verify)

1. **`/flight-plan` senses — read-only, best-effort, re-derived every run, per corpus.** For **each** corpus in the effective list: reads that corpus's HEAD, compares to its last-reconciled SHA, and surfaces any drift under Attention Needed, named by corpus `id`. Because it re-derives from the corpora every run, drift cannot be lost — it reappears in every briefing (and SESSION-BRIEFING) until reconciled. Never hard-fails on a corpus read — but a declared corpus that cannot be read is itself an Attention Needed item ("corpus `<id>` declared but UNOBSERVED"), never a silent omission.
2. **`/compile` reconciles — derive, don't transcribe, per corpus.** When a corpus's HEAD has advanced, it refreshes the wiki's shipped-state projection (Implementation-Status sections, the active flight-plan dashboard, HEALTH) from that corpus by **pull-from-expectation** — the wiki's claims name the corpus artifact they await, and `/compile` checks presence and updates with the corpus SHA as evidence — then advances **that corpus's** last-reconciled SHA. An unreachable corpus's SHA is never advanced, and its HEALTH row records the failed observation.
3. **`/audit` watches the watcher.** It treats the corpus as an evidence source: a "shipped" claim with no corresponding corpus artifact is a contradiction, logged like any other audit finding.

### Rules

- **Never transcribe shipped-state into `raw/`.** Decisions and rationale still flow through `raw/`; only the *shipped-state fact* is corpus-derived.
- **Corpus reads are read-only and best-effort.** No orchestrator may hard-fail because a corpus read failed; degrade and continue.
- **Partial observation is never silent** (v3.0-88). With more than one declared corpus, "the corpus was checked" is only true when **every** corpus was checked. Any consumer that could read some corpora but not others must say so per corpus — in the briefing, the receipt, and the HEALTH row — never report overall health derived from the readable subset alone.
- **Detection is wired to correction.** `/flight-plan` detects (and re-detects every run); `/compile` corrects. The derive-every-run property is the wire — there is no detected-but-forgotten state.

## 17. Derivation Block & v3 Memory-Engine Contract

> **Status:** both the *contract* — schema, determinism sensors, governing invariants — AND the
> *engine* that produces/consumes this state ship with the `knowledge-os` capability. The full
> `deploy/` pipeline (`compile-v2.py`, `staleness.py`, `catalog.py`, `assemble.py`, delta
> registration, the sensor and drill families) was extracted 2026-07-11 from the proving-ground
> fork, where live operations proved the full loop — register → route → compile → cross-vendor
> verify → census — on real content. See `docs/engine/OPERATIONS.md` for how to run it.
> Authoritative spec: `memory-engine-v3-spec.md` (frozen); security/autonomy model:
> `memory-engine-v3-tool-grant-tcb-spec.md` (v1.7) + `memory-engine-v3-autonomy-model-spec.md` (v1.4).

### 17.1 The derivation block (view contract — spec §5)

A wiki article MAY carry a single **engine-managed, mechanically-strippable** region —
the *derivation block* — delimited exactly by:

```
# --- derivation (engine-managed; strip region) ---
...keys...
# --- /derivation ---
```

It is the engine's machine-readable record of how the view was derived. **Humans never
hand-edit it; the engine owns it, and it strips in one pass (reversibility).** When the
block is present, all keys below are required:

| Key | Meaning | Values |
|---|---|---|
| `schema_version` | the block schema version | must equal the sensor's (`3.2`); **skew is refused** |
| `view` | view kind | `topic` \| `dashboard` \| `index` \| `briefing` |
| `summary` | one line for the catalog | free text |
| `entities` | routing entities | list |
| `status` | lifecycle | `active` \| `superseded` |
| `tier` | absorb/verify tier | `T1`..`T4` (T1 = mandatory adversarial verify) |
| `consumed_status` | absorb-audit provenance (F12) | `verified-consumed` \| `legacy-assumed` \| `audit-pending` |
| `origin_max` | max canonical origin of consumed events | e.g. `human`, `corpus`, `external-scrape` (ACC-3 checks it) |
| `subscribes` | entities + corpus this view tracks | `entities: [...]`, `corpus: [<corpus-logical-name>:<artifact-path>]` |
| `bundle` | retrieval closure (catalog-derived) | list of paths |
| `verified` | verify record (null until VERIFY passes; resets on rebuild) | `status` / `at` / `verifier_vendor` / `verifier_model_id` / `absorb_vendor` / `absorb_model_id` / `packet_hash` / `artifact` |

The `subscribes.corpus` value is **ARTIFACT-scoped and instance-configured** — the
corpus logical name resolves from the `project.yaml` corpus binding (`corpus_source` /
`corpus_config`, or a `corpus_sources[]` entry's `id`) at
runtime (never a hardcoded machine path; see § 16). The bracketed form above is an
illustrative placeholder, not a literal default. `tier` uses the project's canonical **four-tier** system (`T1`–`T4`, per `project.yaml` `tier_examples`); the frozen spec §5 example annotates only `T1`/`T3` as illustrations — the sensor accepts all four by design.

### 17.2 Sensors (the determinism layer — `deploy/`)

Shipped with the capability, stdlib-only (no runtime dependency). The shipped set is large
enough (contract sensors, the census, the registration chain, verify/reset sensors, read-path)
that naming every script here would drift the moment one is added or renamed — this section
names the two every instance touches directly and summarizes the rest by family. Per-sensor
contracts live in each script's own docstring; the operating detail lives in
`docs/engine/OPERATIONS.md`.

- **`deploy/check-frontmatter.py`** — validates KNOWN_KEYS across `raw/ wiki/ roadmap/
  receipts/` frontmatter **and** the derivation block, **and detects on-save-formatter
  corruption**: FLATTENed frontmatter (keys reflowed onto one prose line) + collapsed GFM
  table bodies (TABLE-FUSE / TABLE-JAM / TABLE-NOSEP). Sensors *degrade, never block* — with
  one exception: a derivation `schema_version` that does not match the sensor is **refused**
  (skew would silently mis-read machine-critical blocks). `--self-test` runs embedded fixtures;
  `--strict` exits nonzero on any finding (the opt-in pre-commit / CI gate).
- **`deploy/check-derivation.py`** — reports every `audit-pending` **T1** view so the
  dispatch/build path refuses to act on an unaudited top-tier view (F12). `--gate` exits
  nonzero when any is found.
- **Contract/conformance family** (`check-run-diff.py`, `check-phase-gate.py`, `check-golden.py`,
  `check-eco2.py`, `check-caps.py`, `check-path-containment.py`, `check-split.py`,
  `check-autonomy-disposition.py`, `check-journal-sidecar.py`) — per-gate conformance to the
  frozen spec/test-plan; each is `--self-test`-first and gates a specific phase-boundary
  acceptance criterion.
- **Conservation census** (`staleness.py`) — the ACC-1/ACC-2 tripwire (§6): every ledger event
  resolves to exactly one of seven ordered classes; `problems`/`new_holes` is the honesty signal.
  Projects the compile-v2 run journal additively, gated by a chain-integrity check.
- **Registration chain** (`registrations.py`, `register-intake.py`, `backfill-registrations.py`,
  `backfill-derivation.py`, `origin.py`, `check-loop-state.py`) — the typed, append-only
  per-event registration record (§4) and derivation-block backfill; its one-time bulk mint vs.
  incremental delta paths (`docs/engine/OPERATIONS.md`, step 2); the loop-envelope/typed-event
  validator.
- **Verify substrate/reset sensors** (`check-substrate.py`, `check-verified-reset.py`,
  `check-corpus-support.py`, `check-origin-propagation.py`, `routing-census.py`) — the F17/F18
  substrate-gate policy, `verified:` staleness on a re-diverged view, F16 corpus-support
  re-verification at the pinned SHA, and the F15 standalone routing census.
- **Read-path** (`catalog.py`, `assemble.py`, `tool_grant.py`) — ROUTE-1 vocabulary integrity, the
  P4 serving gate (refuses an unverified-T1 view to a build/fix task), and the boot-time
  tool-grant decision behind the origin/co-residency wall (§17.3).
- **Migration-era content audit** (`audit-content.py`, `audit-content-v2.py`) — the enumerate/
  locate verifier-leg harness used when an *existing* corpus migrates onto the engine (see
  RECIPE.md § Day-1 vs. migration); not exercised by fresh-instance steady-state operation.
- **Drills** (`drill-*.py`) — fixture-driven end-to-end rehearsals of the above (concurrency,
  crash-injection, migration replay, workload-cost benchmarking, planted-defect efficacy) — not
  part of the normal compile loop, but the proof each holds under adversarial/load conditions.

Run `check-frontmatter.py --self-test` and `check-derivation.py --self-test` as the
contract-layer self-check before any derivation block is minted.

**Scope (honest).** These are stdlib-only **line-linters**, not full YAML validators: they catch the formatter-corruption + `schema_version`-skew class (the proven corpus-wide killer), not every malformed nesting. They are a **determinism aid, not the security boundary** — the security wall is the boot-time tool-grant (§ 17.3 / `CLAUDE.md` § Security perimeter), which reads the §4 origin register, **not** these sensors. If a security-relevant guarantee is ever required from frontmatter, parse with a real YAML library rather than extending the linter.

### 17.3 Governing invariants (the v3 doctrine)

- **ABSORB / RECONCILE / VERIFY** are the v3 engine verbs (the evolution of `/compile` +
  `/audit`): ABSORB ingests, RECONCILE projects views, VERIFY runs the tiered adversarial
  check that stamps `verified`. Their executable form is built on the fork; the contract
  here is the *state* they read/write (this block + the run journal).
- **Substrate-gate policy (F18):** routine T1 verify gates on `model_id` difference
  (absorb model ≠ verify model); migration-era content audits and design-gate
  verifications gate on **vendor** difference (the cross-substrate firewall).
  `verifier_vendor` and `verifier_model_id` are recorded separately, derived from
  invocation metadata, never orchestrator-supplied strings.
- **Origin & co-residency (boot-time capability isolation):** every view stamps
  `origin_max` = the max canonical origin across its whole lineage; origin can only rise,
  never fall (the ORIGIN-PROPAGATION gate). A session whose boot context carries any
  non-trusted-origin content is **never granted** credential or egress/push tools — the
  wall is *which tools exist in the session at boot*, not a rule the agent is asked to
  follow (see `CLAUDE.md` § Security perimeter; full model in
  `memory-engine-v3-tool-grant-tcb-spec.md` v1.7). The engine's own writes to trusted
  memory are human-in-the-loop by design (`memory-engine-v3-autonomy-model-spec.md` v1.4 § 7). The wall is application-layer tool-grant isolation; the credential-free research session's OS-level secret containment — no host credentials in its process environment — is a separate, named mechanical dependency the engine relies on but does not itself provide.
- **`entities.yaml` + the planned-views registry** are governed **Directory-Preservation
  style** (§ 6): append-only; no entity or planned view is dropped or renamed without
  explicit operator authorization.

### 17.4 Engine state substrates

The engine writes machine state under `receipts/`, alongside the human-authored receipt files
(§ 7). One line each — full contract in `docs/engine/memory-engine-v3-spec.md` § 6:

- **`receipts/journal/`** — the chained run journal (`<seq>.json`, `prev_record_hash`-linked):
  what each compile/verify run absorbed, as set membership — never a watermark.
- **`receipts/registrations/`** — the typed-event registration chain (§ 4): one append-only
  record per ledger event, carrying `origin` / `event_class` / `asserts_corpus_state` for events
  whose own frontmatter can't.
- **`receipts/verify/`** (+ `attest/`, `packets/`) — cross-vendor VERIFY artifacts: the
  assembled packet, the verdict, and the substrate attestation (F17) tying a verdict to a real
  invocation.
- **`receipts/discover/`** — `/discover` sweep receipts (corpus SHA, filed/dismissed findings,
  the standing-lens watchlist).

These are engine-written sidecars, not corpus events — excluded from the conservation census by
a single named exclusion list, never self-registered.

